<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Lusignan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Lusignan was a French noble family from Poitou, lords of Lusignan and counts of La <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marche</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angouleme">Angoulême</a>. Through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-of-lusignan">Guy of Lusignan</a> it gained the crowns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> at the end of the 12th century, and it ruled Cyprus until the late 15th century. A branch inherited the throne of the Armenian Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> in 1342, and the family kept the title King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> after its fall in 1375.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-lusignan.jpg" alt="House of Lusignan" width="320" height="374" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James2813 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_Arms_of_the_House_of_Lusignan_(Kings_of_Armenia,_Cyprus_and_Jerusalem).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The House of Lusignan was a royal house of French origin that ruled kingdoms in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and Armenian Cilicia, from the 12th to the 15th century. It originated at Lusignan in Poitou in the early 10th century, and by the end of the 11th century its castle there made it the most prominent family of petty lords in the region. Hugh VI inherited the County of La Marche in 1091 as a descendant of Almodis of La Marche. In 1200 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabella-of-angouleme">Isabella of Angoulême</a>, to whom Hugh le Brun de Lusignan had been betrothed, depriving Hugh of La Marche.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 1170s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aimery-of-cyprus">Aimery of Lusignan</a>, a younger son of Hugh VIII, arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, expelled from his lands by Richard the Lionheart, then acting Duke of Aquitaine. He married Eschiva, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-of-ibelin">Baldwin of Ibelin</a>, and through the patronage of Agnes of Courtenay became Constable of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Jaffa</a> and later Constable of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a>. He became the first Lusignan king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, and also King of Jerusalem. After he and most of the royal family died, his surviving son Hugh became King of Cyprus in 1205, while Jerusalem passed to Maria of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montferrat">Montferrat</a>.</p><p>Hugh of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, whose maternal grandfather had been Hugh I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, took the name Lusignan and founded the second House of Lusignan; he became King of Cyprus and in 1268 was crowned King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. In 1291 the last remnants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a> were captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-ashraf-khalil">Al-Ashraf Khalil</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Henry fled to Cyprus and under his rule the kingdom prospered; he had the Haute Cour keep written records and turned it from an advisory council into a court that tried criminals. Hugh IV, content to be King of Cyprus alone, commissioned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-boccaccio">Giovanni Boccaccio</a>'s Genealogia deorum gentilium.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 1342 Guy de Lusignan was elected King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and took the name Constantine II. The Lusignans tried to impose Western Catholicism on the people of Armenia, whose state religion was the Armenian Apostolic Church. The leadership largely accepted it, but the peasantry opposed it, and Constantine was killed in an uprising in 1344. Leon V de Lusignan was crowned in 1374, and in 1375 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> army invaded and he surrendered, ending the last fully independent Armenian entity of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. His cousin James I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> claimed the title, uniting it with those of Cyprus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. The Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> annexed Cyprus in the late 15th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “House of Lusignan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1199 the <b>Lusignans</b> held it; Hugh X married Isabella of Angoulême, widow of King John, and joined a failed revolt against Louis IX in 1242.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-la-marche">County of La Marche</a></li><li><blockquote>King James II of Cyprus, with Mamluk support, besieged Famagusta and recovered it in 1464, reuniting the island under the <b>Lusignans</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/genoese-famagusta">Genoese Famagusta</a></li></ul></section>
